INT. TELEPHONE EXCHANGE - 1924 - NIGHT

Brass jacks, cord pairs, a wall of blinking lamps. Gaslight on mahogany. The air smells of hot wire.

CORDELIA ASHWORTH-BELL (20s, headset, high collar) stands at the board plugging cords with practiced speed. Her supervisor, MRS. FENWICK (50s, iron-gray bun, pince-nez), watches from behind with a pocket watch.

CORDELIA
Number, please.

CAPTAIN WARRINGTON (V.O.)
(over line, crackling)
Connect me to the Admiralty. At once.

CORDELIA
One moment, sir.

MRS. FENWICK
Cordelia, that line is reserved.

CORDELIA
Mrs. Fenwick, he said at once.

She plugs the cord. A lamp flares red.

CAPTAIN WARRINGTON (V.O.)
(over line)
Miss, are you there? I hear nothing.

CORDELIA
Sir, the Admiralty is not answering.

MRS. FENWICK
(sharp)
Then you disconnect, child.

CORDELIA
But he'll hear the line go dead, Mrs. Fenwick.

CAPTAIN WARRINGTON (V.O.)
(over line, urgent)
Miss, there is a fire at the harbor. Do you understand? Ring everyone.

Cordelia looks at Mrs. Fenwick. Mrs. Fenwick lowers the watch.

MRS. FENWICK
Ring everyone.

Cordelia seizes a bundle of cords and begins to plug, plug, plug, as the lamps all blaze at once.
